## 9. Greedy marginal allocation and the Readiness Frontier

**Objective.** Minimise expected AOG risk, weighted by archetype penalty and deadline class, for a given spares capital.

**Algorithm.** The analytic model (`gnm.optim.model.ItemModel`) gives expected material AOG hours a year for each part from a hub + outstation (METRIC-lite) model: EBO-based hub wait capped at the expedite time, D0 demand counting the whole wait and D3/D10 only the excess beyond 72/240 h, outstation units saving the AUH→station transport hours for D0 demand, and the engine's seeded repair loop. Starting from a planner floor (P(pipeline < total spares) ≥ 0.10) with P2's count-part parameters as the minimum, the greedy repeatedly adds the single unit — one (PN, station) pair — with the largest reduction in penalty-weighted AOG hours per AED of capital, recording (capital, modelled AOG) after each step.

**When greedy is optimal, and when it is not.** If the objective is a sum of separate terms, one per (PN, station), and each term is decreasing and convex in its stock level (as EBO is), marginal analysis yields allocations on the lower convex hull of the exact frontier. At those budgets it is optimal; between them, an integer knapsack could do slightly better. Two things in this project break separability: hub and station stock interact (the hub delay term), and AOG intervals merge per aircraft. So **greedy is a heuristic here**, chosen for speed, transparency and because each step has a clear "why this unit next" explanation a planner can check.

**Picking candidates and the recommendation.** {{RESULT:frontier.n_candidates}} candidates were frozen: P0, P1 and P2 as references; 16 re-optimised budget points at 0.15–2.5 × P2's allocation capital; two "P2 + greedy additions only" points (I110, I125); a TAT-cut lever and a budget+TAT combination. Each was run through the event engine on the selection seeds (100–119), and the recommendation rule was applied there, before the holdout:

> {{RESULT:frontier.recommendation_rule}}

With P2's realised {{RESULT:frontier.aed_per_aog_hour}} per AOG hour, h* = {{RESULT:frontier.h_star}} AOG hours per AED 1M. The rule picked **{{RESULT:frontier.recommended.id}}** on selection seeds, and the pick is unchanged at AOG penalty ×0.5 and ×2 and carrying cost 15% and 25%. All frozen candidates were then run once on the holdout seeds (1000–1019).

Recommended (holdout): capital {{RESULT:frontier.recommended.capital_aed}}, material AOG {{RESULT:frontier.recommended.aog_hours}} per year (95% CI {{RESULT:frontier.recommended.aog_hours_ci}}), versus {{RESULT:frontier.recommended.selection_aog_hours}} on the selection seeds. Paired against P2 on the holdout: {{RESULT:frontier.recommended_vs_P2.delta_aog_hours}} {{RESULT:frontier.recommended_vs_P2.delta_aog_hours_ci}} for {{RESULT:frontier.recommended_vs_P2.delta_capital_aed}} of capital. Analytic model versus simulation across the frontier points: {{RESULT:policy.calibration.predicted_vs_simulated}}.

**Levers beyond buying stock.** The frontier also prices alternatives per part (`outputs/frontier/levers.csv`): buying 1 or 2 more units, paying the vendor to cut TAT by 10 days (AED 400 per unit per day, ASSUMED), and repositioning an existing unit. Pool access is not priced (P3 is not implemented). A tornado (selection seeds, I125 paired against P2 and P0) varies the AOG penalty (×0.5, ×2) and carrying cost (15%, 25%) by re-costing the simulated KPIs, and repair TAT (×0.75, ×1.25) and purchase lead time (×0.75, ×1.25) by re-simulating; a separate run switches the AOG-desk emergency purchase off: {{RESULT:frontier.aog_desk.summary}}.

